Ahnii!

This post shows you how to move a small app from [Laravel Mix](https://laravel-mix.com/) to [Vite](https://vite.dev/) so you can get faster rebuilds with less build config.

## Prerequisites

- A project that still builds assets with Laravel Mix
- Node.js and your package manager installed
- One CSS entry file and one JavaScript entry file

## Replace the old build scripts

Start by removing the old build dependency, but keep your entry files where they are. You want a smaller migration surface first. That makes the next config change easier to verify.

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "vite build"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "vite": "^5.4.0"
  }
}
```

This swaps your project to Vite's development server and production build command. It also gives you a clean place to check whether the migration works before you touch templates.

## Add a Vite config that matches your current entry points

If your old toolchain already had clear entry files, keep that shape. You do not need a bigger rewrite just to change bundlers.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      input: {
        app: resolve(__dirname, 'resources/js/app.js'),
        styles: resolve(__dirname, 'resources/css/app.css'),
      },
    },
  },
});
```

This tells Vite to build the same JavaScript and CSS files your project already uses. That keeps the migration focused on the toolchain instead of mixing it with a file layout change.

## Verify the new workflow

Run the new commands and check the output before you remove any leftover Mix files. If the build succeeds, you can clean up with confidence.

```bash
npm install
npm run build
```

This installs Vite and runs a production build with your new config. If you get output files for both entry points, your migration path is working and cleanup is the next step.

